alty against everything. Light, it turns out, is very stubborn.\n\nInside, everything was grey: grey tapestries, grey servants, a grey throne where the Thorn Witch sat, holding the stolen starlight of the Whispering Glade in a jar. 'Mine,' the Thorn Witch hissed. 'All mine.'"}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'Starlight Restored', 'text': "the Whispering Glade celebrated: the fireflies spelled Willow's name, the oak dropped acorns of thanks, the wise owl composed a ballad with a brave mouse as the hero — a brave mouse insisted Willow was the hero; they compromised on both.\n\nthe Thorn Witch's grumble was kept in a jar on the mantel, as a reminder. On quiet nights it mutters. Willow finds it comforting: proof that dark can be made small, and kept.\n\na crystal leaf chose to stay with Willow — or Willow chose to stay with it; by now they weren't sure there was a difference. It sits by the bed, glowing softly, a nightlight of pure story."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'The Promise Kept', 'text': "New children come to hear the story, and Willow tells it true: the fear, the laughter, the moment it almost went wrong. 'Were you scared?' they ask. 'Terrified,' Willow says. 'That's what brave is.'\n\nthe Whispering Glade thrives: the enchanted forest enchants, the glade whispers kindly, the meadow moonlights. All because one child picked up a crystal leaf and one friend said 'together.'\n\nWillow grew, as heroes do, but never grew out of the Whispering Glade. Every spring they check the old oak. Every night a crystal leaf glows. Every tale a brave mouse tells starts, 'When we saved the stars...'"}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Bedtime', 'text': "'Again,' says the listener. It is always 'again.' The best stories are.\n\nWillow smiles from the doorway. Outside, the stars are out — all of them, every one, back where they belong. The brook sings. The fireflies dance.\n\nAnd now it's bedtime in the Whispering Glade, and a crystal leaf glows soft, and a brave mouse is telling the story one more time, to one more 