ries, grey servants, a grey throne where the Thorn Witch sat, holding the stolen starlight of the Hollow Hill in a jar. 'Mine,' the Thorn Witch hissed. 'All mine.'"}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'Starlight Restored', 'text': "a moonstone chose to stay with Ember — or Ember chose to stay with it; by now they weren't sure there was a difference. It sits by the bed, glowing softly, a nightlight of pure story.\n\nthe Thorn Witch's grumble was kept in a jar on the mantel, as a reminder. On quiet nights it mutters. Ember finds it comforting: proof that dark can be made small, and kept.\n\nthe Hollow Hill celebrated: the fireflies spelled Ember's name, the oak dropped acorns of thanks, the wise owl composed a ballad with a brave mouse as the hero — a brave mouse insisted Ember was the hero; they compromised on both."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'The Promise Kept', 'text': "New children come to hear the story, and Ember tells it true: the fear, the laughter, the moment it almost went wrong. 'Were you scared?' they ask. 'Terrified,' Ember says. 'That's what brave is.'\n\nlove breaks every spell. In the Hollow Hill, in the starlight, in every heart that hears the tale: the promise kept, the story told, the magic real.\n\na brave mouse is older now, greyer, no less brave. They still walk together, still argue about the ballad, still keep the grumble-jar dusted. Friendship, like starlight, doesn't fade if you tend it."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Bedtime', 'text': "'Again,' says the listener. It is always 'again.' The best stories are.\n\nlove breaks every spell. Goodnight.\n\nEmber smiles from the doorway. Outside, the stars are out — all of them, every one, back where they belong. The brook sings. The fireflies dance."}]}

